     permission, Android 12 behaviour changes) on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A medication reminder fires at 8:00 sharp on every test phone. In the field, users report it arriving at 9:30, or not until they pick up the phone. The test phones were on a desk, plugged in, screen on. The users' phones were asleep on a nightstand, where Android had been deferring the alarm, on purpose, to save battery.</p>

Android assumes background work can wait, and enforces it with **Doze**, which defers work when the device is idle, and **App Standby Buckets**, which ration work by how recently each app was used. Work that truly cannot wait has to use one of the narrow exits the system provides. This chapter shows how idle phases unfold, what is deferred in each, and which exit fits which feature.

<!-- @after First Principles :: animation -->
### A night on the nightstand

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Doze phases and what still runs</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node is-key" data-el="phase"><strong>Device state</strong><small data-text="phaseV">—</small></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:3"><div class="node" data-el="jobs"><strong>Jobs and syncs</strong><small data-text="jobsV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="alarms"><strong>Alarms</strong><small data-text="alarmsV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="net"><strong>Network</strong><small data-text="netV">—</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"In use","caption":"Screen on or charging: no Doze restrictions. This is the state most tests run in.","add":["phase","jobs","alarms","net"],"state":{"phase":"done","jobs":"done","alarms":"done","net":"done"},"text":{"phaseV":"active","jobsV":"run on schedule","alarmsV":"fire on time","netV":"available"}},
{"label":"Asleep","caption":"Unplugged, screen off and stationary for a while: the device enters Doze. Jobs and syncs are deferred, network access is suspended, wake locks are ignored, and standard alarms wait.","state":{"phase":"warn","jobs":"ghost","alarms":"ghost","net":"ghost"},"text":{"phaseV":"Doze","jobsV":"deferred","alarmsV":"deferred, including setExact","netV":"suspended"}},
{"label":"Maintenance window","caption":"Periodically the system opens a short window: deferred jobs, syncs and alarms run together, then Doze resumes. Windows become less frequent the longer the device stays idle.","state":{"phase":"active","jobs":"active","alarms":"active","net":"active"},"text":{"phaseV":"maintenance window","jobsV":"batched run","alarmsV":"deferred ones fire","netV":"briefly available"}},
{"label":"Back to sleep","caption":"The window closes. Anything the app did not finish waits for the next one, which is why the reminder arrived at 9:30.","state":{"phase":"warn","jobs":"ghost","alarms":"ghost","net":"ghost"},"text":{"phaseV":"Doze","jobsV":"deferred","alarmsV":"deferred","netV":"suspended"}},
{"label":"The narrow exits","caption":"A high-priority FCM message can wake the app briefly to show a notification; setExactAndAllowWhileIdle can fire an alarm during Doze, rate-limited and permission-gated. Everything else waits.","state":{"alarms":"done","net":"warn"},"text":{"alarmsV":"setExactAndAllowWhileIdle fires","netV":"short access for a high-priority FCM message"}}
]</script><figcaption>Design for the nightstand, not the desk: assume work waits, and use an exit only when the user would notice the delay.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @diagram 195946fb412e3416 -->
### How a device reaches Doze

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-stack"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Screen off</strong></div><div class="node"><strong>Unplugged</strong></div><div class="node"><strong>Stationary</strong></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Timer expires → Doze</strong></div></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:3"><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Network off</strong><small>nothing transferred</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Alarms deferred</strong><small>wake locks ignored</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Jobs queued</strong><small>WorkManager waits</small></div></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Maintenance window</strong><small>brief, batched wake-up; then back to Doze</small></div></div><figcaption>The restrictions apply to every app at once; maintenance windows are the shared moments when deferred work runs.</figcaption></figure>

